  'help.html':
    '<h3>Photo → review → GSI</h3><p>The steps at the top show where you are: Photo, Frame, Review, File. On a phone, the button at the bottom always runs the next step.</p><p>Add photos or screenshots of tables. The app looks for the table edges; check the frame on the “Frame” tab. All rows you need must stay inside. If necessary, drag the corners, rotate the photo or choose “Whole photo”. Then tap “Recognize photo”.</p><h3>Recognition</h3><p>Tables with a clear grid are read cell by cell. Without a grid the app reads whole lines. The E/N order comes from the headings Rechtswert/Hochwert or Easting/Northing; if no headings are recognized, E, N is used and a message is shown. You can also set the order yourself before recognition.</p><p>Cleaning helps with photos of screens but can make small or faint text worse. In that case, turn it off and recognize again. Recognition only replaces the points of the selected photo. Misread digits are never guessed or corrected automatically.</p><h3>Review every point</h3><p>Tap the circle next to a point. Above each field you see the matching cell of the <b>original</b> photo, not the cleaned copy. Check the name, sign, every digit and the decimal separator, then tap “Reviewed · next”. Any change resets the review status. The photo may contain more rows than were recognized: always compare the number of points and look at the recognition result.</p><h3>Coordinate rules</h3><ul><li>All values are in metres. Negative values, decimal point and decimal comma are supported. On iPhone the ± button changes the sign.</li><li><code>3.415.030,210</code>, <code>3,415,030.210</code> and <code>3 415 030,210</code> can be typed or pasted from Excel.</li><li>Up to 4 decimal places. With 4 decimals the GSI export is written in 0.1 mm (unit code 6), otherwise in millimetres. More than 4 decimals block the export: decide yourself how to round. There is no automatic rounding.</li><li>An empty height is not written to the GSI; an explicit <code>0</code> is written as a zero height.</li><li>Names: up to 16 characters, Latin letters, digits, dot, hyphen and underscore. Spaces are removed. Names that differ only in leading zeros or letter case count as duplicates.</li><li>Coordinates are not converted between systems: DB_REF, GK or UTM stay as in the source.</li></ul><h3>Format and CAPLAN</h3><p>Export: standard Leica GSI-16, ASCII, CRLF line endings, words 11/81/82/83. Load the file in your software as Leica GSI coordinate data. Check your CAPLAN version and import settings with the first file: E, N, height and names must match. The export contains no station measurements and no field codes.</p><h3>Saving and privacy</h3><p>Photos and points are processed in the browser and saved automatically in a local draft. They are not synced between devices. “Save project” downloads a JSON file with photos, points and recognition results. Opening such a file resets the review status. Clearing the browser data also deletes the draft — save important projects as files.</p><h3>iPhone and offline</h3><p>Open the app in Safari and add it to the Home Screen via “Share”. Then tap “Offline” and download the required files. The first start and loading the module need internet; offline readiness is confirmed only after the download has finished. iOS may delete the cache when storage runs low.</p><h3>Supported sources</h3><p>JPG, PNG and WebP; other formats if your browser can read them. If HEIC does not open, use JPG or a screenshot. PDF is not supported directly: add a screenshot of the table. Up to 20 photos of 20 MB each; a long side over 2400 pixels is reduced. Intended for printed tables, not for handwriting or whole drawings.</p><hr><p>Recognition engine: Tesseract.js {version} (Apache-2.0). <a href="./licenses.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Component licenses</a>.</p>',
